{"id":"loan-amended-and-restated","title":"Housing loans amended, restated or enlarged","question":"How often does a City housing loan come back to the Board to be amended, restated, enlarged or rolled into a new loan, and on what terms?","finding":"Of 80 housing-loan files the Board passed since 2015, 41 change an earlier loan: 34 amend and restate it, 2 consolidate or restructure loans, and 5 state an increase, $31.7 million between them. 25 files state the loan’s term; the typical term is 57 years. The property file measured the same thing by address and found 5 developments whose loan limit rose between files, by $40.1 million in all. No file on disk forgives a housing loan by name, and none records a repayment.","how":"Board resolutions and ordinances passed since 2015 whose short title names a loan and whose long title names the housing office or affordable housing (State loans the City merely accepts, bond issues and settlements are left out). What each file does is read from its title: “amended and restated”, “amendment”, “increase the loan amount by”, “consolidated”. The amount is the title’s “not to exceed” figure; the term is the title’s “loan term of N years”. Newest first; files that neither change an earlier loan nor state a term are counted but not listed.","limits":"Replacing an early loan with a later, larger one for the same building need not mean trouble: the City’s financial report lists shorter-term predevelopment and acquisition loans beside the long-term ones, and the Board votes each time. The same report says such loans are repaid only from a project’s residual receipts, which goes with terms this long. Of the 5 titles that state an increase, 5 give additional construction or rehabilitation costs as the reason and 2 add “loss of permanent financing”. The titles do not give the interest rate, what was repaid on the earlier loan, or whether any payment has ever been made. What would settle that: the loan agreements’ payment terms and the housing office’s record of residual-receipt payments received. Amounts, parties and dates taken from Board file titles are read by machine: in a hand check of 50 titles, 11 had at least one field read wrongly (4 after later fixes).","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"passed","t":"Passed","type":"date"},{"k":"borrower","t":"Borrower","type":"org"},{"k":"does","t":"What the file does","type":"text"},{"k":"amount","t":"Loan limit in the title","type":"usd"},{"k":"increase","t":"Increase stated","type":"usd"},{"k":"term","t":"Term stated (years)","type":"int"},{"k":"file","t":"Board file","type":"link"}],"rows":[{"passed":"2026-10-02","does":"amends and restates an earlier loan; amends a loan","amount":16083595,"increase":null,"term":null,"file":{"t":"File 260968","u":"https://sfgov.legistar.com/LegislationDetail.aspx?ID=8201435&GUID=B0B943C9-5256-4FF4-B7FE-C7297C15EF0F"},"borrower":"3300 Mission Partners L.P.","borrowerKey":null},{"passed":"2026-07-30","does":"amends a loan","amount":13666153,"increase":null,"term":null,"file":{"t":"File 260793","u":"https://sfgov.legistar.com/LegislationDetail.aspx?ID=8132896&GUID=261966F5-1AB9-4653-A821-1720995E39A5"},"borrower":"A borrower not named in the short title","borrowerKey":null},{"passed":"2026-07-23","does":"amends a loan","amount":27248500,"increase":null,"term":null,"file":{"t":"File 260773","u":"https://sfgov.legistar.com/LegislationDetail.aspx?ID=8127459&GUID=AE8D7CB0-1086-4C6F-A77D-9F9D91D23B90"},"borrower":"1234 Great Highway LLC","borrowerKey":null},{"passed":"2026-06-11","does":"amends and restates an earlier loan","amount":22578308,"increase":null,"term":null,"file":{"t":"File 260547","u":"https://sfgov.legistar.com/LegislationDetail.aspx?ID=8037415&GUID=C4740479-98BF-4F02-9464-459AF04C52DD"},"borrower":"A borrower not named in the short title","borrowerKey":null},{"passed":"2026-04-10","does":"amends and restates an earlier loan","amount":29280757,"increase":null,"term":57,"file":{"t":"File 260242","u":"https://sfgov.